SOURCE @addassi1998
  description: Policy development for in-situ burning as an oil spill response method in California's marine environment, addressing multi-agency coordination, stakeholder consensus, and public perception challenges.
  epistemic_model: Policy Studies
  method: Working group formation, consensus approach to policy development
END SOURCE

ITEM @addassi1998
  text: The use of burning as an available oil spill response method can play an important role in reducing the possible widespread impact of oil spills to marine resources
  note: Explicit mechanism: In-situ burning reduces environmental damage through direct response capability that limits spill spread to sensitive marine resources.
  chain: In-situ_Burning -> Impact_Reduction
END ITEM

ITEM @addassi1998
  text: The use of this technology, however, often requires the approval of many different oversight agencies
  note: *borderline* Direction only: The excerpt supports Regulatory_Approval as prerequisite for Technology_Use.
  chain: Regulatory_Approval -> Technology_Use
END ITEM

SOURCE @agaton2020
  description: Analyzes the economic, environmental, and social impacts of adopting electric vehicles for public transportation in the Philippines, examining obstacles and benefits of the transition from conventional to electric transport systems.
  epistemic_model: Implicit: Sustainability Assessment (triple-bottom-line framework)
  method: case study
END SOURCE

ITEM @agaton2020
  text: the adoption of electric vehicles faces several obstacles ranging from financing issues, government policies, and public acceptance
  note: *complex* Three-factor convergence (Financing, Policy, Public_Acceptance) indicates adoption requires integrated multi-domain intervention. Direction only: Financing constraints limit adoption.
  chain: Financing -> EV_Adoption
END ITEM

